    not_duplicate_of: []  # 64f7
    pr_ref:  # 64f7
    description: "FOUND 2026-09-03 by the sweep INV-nopoh's own filing required (\"sweep for the same\nshape in the other tree-sitter analyzers -- verify by fixture, not by reading\"),\nrun on dev 7cf2cf411a BEFORE the Go fix. Fixtures and runner:\n/tmp/claude-1002/-home-jgstern-agent-hypergumbo/5338fdcf-d75c-4d63-a74c-d21b2c0a702d/scratchpad/sweep/\n(copied to ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/nopoh_pkglit_09032026/sweep/ with the PR).\n\nTHE SHAPE. One source file per language with the same three sites: a call inside a\nfunction literal bound at MODULE/PACKAGE level (PKG_LITERAL), a call inside a\nliteral held by a module-level container or field initializer (PKG_FIELD_LITERAL /\nSTATIC_INIT / PKG_INIT_CALL), and -- as the positive control -- the same call inside\na literal inside a NAMED function (IN_NAMED). For each site: did a `calls` edge with\nthat line exist, and on which symbol was it anchored?\n\n    language  PKG_LITERAL         PKG_FIELD/INIT        IN_NAMED (control)\n    rust      NONE                --                    named:function\n    java      NONE                STATIC_INIT NONE      M.named:method\n    kotlin    NONE                NONE                  named:function\n    swift     NONE                --                    named:function\n    ruby      NONE                NONE                  named:method\n    c         --                  PKG_INIT NONE (C++ only in practice)  named:function\n    cpp       NONE                PKG_INIT NONE         named:function\n    scala     NONE                --                    M.named:method\n    lua       NONE                NONE                  named:function\n    csharp    NONE                --                    NONE  (see below)\n    -- already anchored, for contrast --\n    js        handler:function    file:file             inner;named\n    ts        handler:function    cmd:variable          inner;named\n    py        file:file           file:file             named:function\n    php       file:file           file:file             named:function\n    go        NONE -> fixed by INV-nopoh (variable symbol; file for `var _`)\n\nEvery control emits; every package-level site in the nine languages emits nothing.\nThis is INV-foluz's invariant (\"every call site the analyzer parses emits a calls\nedge anchored on SOME symbol\") violated at the same place in nine analyzers: each\nwalks up to a function/method node and returns None at the file root.\n\nTHE INSTANCES THAT MATTER MOST, by likely volume (NOT measured on a corpus -- that\nis the first step of any fix):\n  rust    `static X: LazyLock<T> = LazyLock::new(|| { ... })`, `lazy_static!`,\n          `once_cell::Lazy::new(|| ...)` -- config loading and I/O at first use.\n  java    static field initializers and `static { ... }` blocks (loggers, clients).\n  kotlin  top-level `val x = ...` and `by lazy { ... }`.\n  swift   global `let x = { ... }()` and top-level statements in main.swift.\n  ruby    top-level code is the NORM (scripts, Rakefiles, config/*.rb).\n  scala   `object` bodies: every `val` in an object is a package-level initializer.\n  cpp     global `static auto x = f();` and lambdas held in globals.\n  lua     module-level code is the norm (every `require`-d module runs at load).\n\nCSHARP IS A DIFFERENT DEFECT. The csharp analyzer emitted ZERO calls edges even\ninside the named method for `File.ReadAllText(\"z\")`; a second fixture shows it\nemits an edge for a call to a LOCAL method (`M.Named -> M.Helper`) but none for an\nexternal/unresolved callee, and none at all from the static-field lambda even for\n`Helper()`. So csharp has (a) no unresolved-callee emission (INV-foluz's Python\nshape, which `make_unresolved_edge` exists to fix) and (b) this item's shape.\nRecorded here; not folded -- (a) is a separate invariant and probably its own item.\n\nWHAT THE FIX IS, per INV-nopoh's reference implementation (go.py):\n  1. Find the analyzer's enclosing-scope walk (the `_get_enclosing_function` twin).\n  2. When the walk reaches a module-level declaration that already has a symbol\n     (a `variable`/`constant`/`field` the symbol pass emits), anchor on it.\n  3. When it reaches the file root with no symbol, anchor on the file pseudo-symbol\n     (the Python `<module>` convention) -- never return None for a parsed call.\n  4. Where var_types / receiver typing is scoped by the same walk's NAME, the two\n     walks must return the same name (go.py: `_go_package_level_var_name`, one\n     helper, both callers) or a typed local in a module-level literal is recorded\n     under one key and looked up under another.\n  5. A/B on a real repo per language, read back every new anchor by callee (the Go\n     read-back found grouped `var ( ... )` blocks had NO symbols at all -- the fix\n     exposed a second symbol-pass defect; expect the same).\n  6. Check the DDG side: `LanguageDdgSpec.function_node_types` will not CFG a\n     literal body under a module-level declaration, so new findings anchored there\n     are `structural` only (WI filed with INV-nopoh for Go; same for each language).\n\nNOT THIS ITEM: attributing a literal's calls to the enclosing NAMED function where\none exists -- that is the existing, kept behaviour in every language above."  # 64f7
    fields:  # 64f7
      statement: "Every call site an analyzer parses emits a calls edge anchored on SOME symbol (INV-foluz). A call inside a module/package-level function literal or initializer emits nothing in nine tree-sitter analyzers; the same site inside a named function emits in every one of them."  # 64f7
      root_cause: "Each analyzer's enclosing-scope walk looks for a function/method node and returns None at the file root, and the call arm skips a call with no enclosing symbol. Go's fix (INV-nopoh): anchor on the module-level declaration's own symbol, else the file pseudo-symbol; both scope walks (anchor and var_types) share one helper."  # 64f7
